+## v2 service: several Frigate detector processes, batching (2026-09-06)
+
+Full measurements: `~/gpu-opt/PHASE3_DETECTOR.md`. Short version:
+
+* **Frigate never has two requests in flight on one detector process** (pcap of the live service: 0 % of requests
+  arrive while another is being served; even back-to-back requests are 13.5 ms apart, p50, because that is what
+  Frigate's detector process spends per request on dequeue / preprocess / `tobytes` / publish). The only way to
+  overlap that with the GPU, without patching the plugin, is to list the same endpoint **twice** in Frigate's
+  `detectors:` (`host_zmq` and `host_zmq_b` in `config/config.yaml`): Frigate feeds all detector processes from one
+  shared queue, so two requests can be in flight. Each process handshakes separately and after every timeout.
+* The service is therefore a **ROUTER** socket now (`zmq_onnx_client.py` v2; REQ clients need no change, replies to
+  vanished clients are dropped), drains everything queued in the socket after each inference, answers a handshake
+  for the already-loaded file without reloading it (the old REP service reloaded on every handshake; two clients
+  handshaking against it produced a reload storm that held Frigate at 190-250 ms / 0 detections for 2 minutes),
+  takes the tensor straight from the ZMQ frame (`copy=False`, `np.frombuffer`), and logs per-stage timings in its
+  `Inference stats` line (`DETECTOR_STATS_EVERY` requests, default 1000).
+* Knobs: `--max-batch N` / `DETECTOR_MAX_BATCH` (queued requests coalesced into one inference; `MnnBackend` then
+  builds sessions for 1, 2, 4 .. N from fixed-batch model variants) and `--batch-window-ms` /
+  `DETECTOR_BATCH_WINDOW_MS` (wait that long after the first queued request for more; 0 = never wait).
+  **Production uses `--max-batch 1`**: on this GPU a batch of 2/4/8 costs 2.1/3.1/6.2x a single frame (engine
+  12.0 -> 25.3 / 37.3 / 74.5 ms, 9.3 ms/frame at best), so batching lowered throughput headroom by ~20 % at the price
+  of 10-25 ms more latency per request in every load pattern tried, and produced the only timeouts of the sweep.
+  With `--max-batch 1` the batch-1 path is bit-identical to the 10:55 service (same `.mnn`, same session).
+* Batch>1 correctness needed: sessions created at their batch size (a session *resized* to batch>1 on the Vulkan
+  backend computes garbage or segfaults) and the YOLO DFL head rewritten in the batch variants (MNN flattens its
+  last-axis softmax to `N*8400` image rows, over the driver's 16384-row limit from N=2). With that, batch 2/4/8 give
+  the same 36/36 detections as batch 1 on the 45-frame set (score <= 0.003, box <= 0.37 px), not bit-identical.
+* Round trip: service-side Python is ~1.1 ms per request (`cProfile`); engine 11.2-12 ms quiet, 14-15 ms next to the
+  NVR; the rest of the ~18 ms round trip is the 1.23 MB tensor on TCP (0.9 ms on the wire) and the client. Shared
+  memory or uint8 tensors would need the Frigate plugin patched; not done.
+* Measured with 4 staggered clients at 5 fps (20 req/s, what 4 cameras look like): 18.9 ms avg, 28.3 ms p95, 0
+  timeouts, service at 6-7 % of a core. With 4 clients firing in lock-step: 43.8 / 66.6 ms (pure queueing).
+  10-minute soak (4 x 3 fps, lib-v2): 7204 requests, 0 timeouts/mismatches, 21.4 ms avg / 32.2 p95 / 56.4 max,
+  RSS flat at 158 MB, dmesg clean. Production after the switch (2026-09-06 14:08, real load): engine 13.4-14.5 ms
+  avg, queue-wait max 1.8 ms, Frigate `inference_speed` 24-27 ms per process, 0 timeouts in 15 min.
+
+Rollback of this step alone: `detectors:` back to the single `host_zmq` (`docker compose restart frigate`), and/or the
+unit's `DETECTOR_MNN_LIB` back to `mnn-batch/lib/libMNN.so`; `--max-batch 1` is already the sequential behaviour. The
+previous unit is `research/frigate-detector.service.before-v2` and the previous config
+`config/config.yaml.bak.<date>-v2`. **Never run `loadgen.py`/`test_client.py` with more than one client against the
+live port**; use a second instance on 5556.
 
